# nexfortis-content-pipeline repo conventions

This is the **NexFortis Automated Content Pipeline v2** — backend automation
only. No UI, no React, no frontend frameworks. pnpm workspace monorepo,
TypeScript strict, **Node.js 22+**, pnpm 10. The full agent-facing guide is in
[AGENTS.md](mdc:AGENTS.md). Read it before any implementation work.

## Hard rules — do not violate

- **One prompt = one PR.** Every implementation task comes from a prompt in
  [`docs/ways-of-work/plan/content-pipeline-v2/cursor-claude-prompt-library.md`](mdc:docs/ways-of-work/plan/content-pipeline-v2/cursor-claude-prompt-library.md).
  Open one PR per prompt. Do not combine prompts.
- **Stay inside each prompt's allowed-file list.** Each prompt declares the
  exact files it may create or modify and the exact files it must not touch.
  Do not refactor adjacent code, do not consolidate utilities, do not
  auto-format files outside the allowed list.
- **Never edit context files**: `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, anything under
  `.cursor/`, `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md`, `.editorconfig`,
  `.gitignore`. If a behavior change is needed, stop and add a
  `// TODO(hassan):` note in the PR description — do not modify the context
  file.
- **`docs/` is read-only.** Specs are the source of truth — code follows
  specs, not the other way around. If a spec is wrong, stop and flag it; do
  not change code to match a wrong spec or change a spec to match wrong code.
- **Use pnpm.** `npm` and `yarn` are blocked by the `preinstall` hook.
- **Branch protection on `main`** — all work goes through PRs, squash merges
  only, CI must pass.
- **No `.env` files committed.**
- **No new top-level dependencies** without an explicit allowlist entry in the
  active prompt. Stop and ask if a dependency is needed but not allowed.
- **No dependency version bumps** beyond what the prompt allows.
- **No changes to `lib/db` schemas** unless the active prompt explicitly names
  the table being changed.
- **No PII or family-law content in the corpus.** The `lib/redaction` module
  is fail-closed. Use synthetic fixtures for testing — never real captures.
- **Use the words "collect", "extract", "browse", "read"** when describing
  fetching pages or data. Do not use "scrape" or "crawl" anywhere.

## Stack conventions

- TypeScript strict mode. No `any`. Use `unknown` and narrow.
- Exported shared types live in `lib/shared-types`. Service-local types stay
  in the service.
- Discriminated unions for multi-state values.
- All async wrapped in try/catch with structured logging via `lib/logger`
  (pino + `{ correlationId, source, action }` context).
- Idempotency keys on every external write.
- Drizzle ORM. No raw SQL except in migrations.
- Inngest for cron + async events. No setTimeout/setInterval for scheduling.
- Vitest for tests. Path: `tests/<service>/<test-name>.test.ts`. Happy path +
  2 edge cases per requirement, minimum.

## Build and test

- `pnpm install`
- `pnpm typecheck`
- `pnpm test`
- `pnpm lint`
- `pnpm build`

All four must pass before opening a PR.

## PR conventions

- **Title:** `Prompt N: <short description>`
- **Description:** follows `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md`
- **Definition of Done** items from the prompt all checked off before
  requesting review
- Hassan is the merge approver; Computer (the strategist AI) co-reviews
